EXT. CRANBERRY BOG - BEFORE DAWN

A flooded harvest bog, a rectangle of black water under a sky the color of dishwater. Ten thousand red berries float on the surface, a drifting crimson carpet corralled by yellow booms. Mist moves in low skeins. Far off a pump engine thumps.

VIVIAN KESTREL, 40s, in chest waders and a stolen foreman's jacket, wades waist-deep through the berries with a dry bag held overhead. The berries part around her and close again behind, leaving no wake. She moves like a woman who has learned that patience is a kind of weight.

She stops at the center of the bog. She looks at the sky, then at the far levee, and mutters.

VIVIAN
Come on. Come on, you old goat.

Nothing answers. A heron lifts from the reeds and flaps away. She waits, and sweat runs down her temple though the water is cold.

A pair of headlights crawl along the levee road, a mile out. They slow, stop, go dark. A door shuts. The pump engine stutters and cuts out and the silence is sudden and total.

VIVIAN (CONT'D)
(to the dark)
Twelve minutes, I told you. Twelve.

A figure picks his way down the levee bank, flashlight beam bouncing. LEOPOLD ASANTE, 60s, heavy, in a tweed coat that has no business in a bog, slides on the last six feet of mud and lands on his hands. The flashlight goes in the water. It sinks glowing, shrinks, and vanishes.

LEOPOLD
That was a good flashlight.

VIVIAN
You were supposed to come alone.

LEOPOLD
I came alone. The flashlight was a second guest.

He wades out, holding his coat above the waterline with both fists, grimacing at every step. The berries bump his knees. At ten feet he stops, breathing hard.

LEOPOLD (CONT'D)
You have it?

VIVIAN
I have what you paid for.

LEOPOLD
That's not an answer.

VIVIAN
It's the only one you get until I see the envelope.

He reaches into the coat. Takes out a plastic freezer bag, fat with cash, and holds it up. The bag shines dull yellow in the thin light. She lowers the dry bag and unzips the top.

For a moment neither moves. Then Vivian's hand goes into the bag and Leopold's hand goes deeper into his coat, and both of them stop at once, because somewhere on the levee a dog barks.

VIVIAN (CONT'D)
(low)
Whose dog?

LEOPOLD
Not mine.

VIVIAN
Whose dog, Leopold?

LEOPOLD
I don't know! Farmers have dogs!

The barking intensifies, joined by a second dog. Headlights flick on at the levee's far end, then another pair behind. Vivian sees them first. She throws the dry bag at Leopold, who catches it against his chest and goes down backward into the berries, which close over him in a red bloom.

VIVIAN (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Stupid, stupid, stupid.

She grabs his collar, hauls him up streaming and spitting, and shoves the freezer bag into her jacket. He struggles against her and they turn together in the berries, grappling, her elbow in his ribs, his knee in the water, until she plants a boot and pulls him bodily toward the reeds. They crash through the booms, scattering a hundred yards of floating berries, and fall into the sedge on the far side of the bog with the dry bag between them.

LEOPOLD
(coughing)
You pushed me.

VIVIAN
I saved you.

LEOPOLD
You pushed me in a bog.

VIVIAN
It's a saving push.

The dogs reach the water's edge and stop, baying. Flashlights sweep the surface. A man's voice calls, indistinct, bored, tired. Leopold and Vivian lie flat in the sedge, shoulder to shoulder, not breathing, mud on their faces, red berries stuck in their hair like ornaments.

The flashlights linger. A long minute. Then a truck door slams, an engine starts, and the headlights draw away down the levee.

The two lie still a while longer. The mist closes in. The first thin light comes across the water and turns the berries to coral.

LEOPOLD
(at last)
We'll never do this again.

VIVIAN
No.

LEOPOLD
It was a bad idea.

VIVIAN
The worst.

She sits up and unzips the dry bag. Inside is a small velvet case. She opens it, and the dawn catches whatever is in there, a cold white fire, and for the first time that morning Vivian smiles.

LEOPOLD
(softly)
Is it all right?

VIVIAN
It's perfect.

Leopold takes the freezer bag from her jacket, unsealed, and counts the bills with wet fingers. A berry has got into the bag. He fishes it out and eats it.

LEOPOLD
Tart.

VIVIAN
They always are.

She closes the case. He holds out his hand and she shakes it, solemn, absurd, both of them red to the elbows. Then they stand and wade away in opposite directions through the drifting fruit.
